WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.260 --> 00:00:07.519
And now, for our final section on the interface, let's go over variables.

2
00:00:07.519 --> 00:00:13.179
You'll be able to find the variables section down here in the bottom left-hand corner.

3
00:00:13.179 --> 00:00:20.690
Now, variables are just an area in memory which has been allocated to save something.

4
00:00:20.690 --> 00:00:26.289
Now, the thing that can be saved to it can be a lot of different things.

5
00:00:26.289 --> 00:00:31.010
At the moment, we have three variables down here in the bottom left-hand corner,

6
00:00:31.089 --> 00:00:34.450
and they are all references, as shown by blue,

7
00:00:34.450 --> 00:00:38.689
and they're references to the components that we already have within our actor.

8
00:00:39.820 --> 00:00:47.899
Now, if I push plus, this is going to create a new variable, and this is of type Boudia.

9
00:00:48.689 --> 00:00:54.289
Now, if we come into the top right-hand corner, we can see here that we've got the details.

10
00:00:54.289 --> 00:00:56.929
The first thing is we've got the name. This can be anything.

11
00:00:57.729 --> 00:01:00.369
And under that, we have variable type.

12
00:01:02.350 --> 00:01:08.829
If we do the drop down from the variable type, we're going to see a number of different options.

13
00:01:09.469 --> 00:01:13.629
The first one we have here is a Boolean, and we talked about this before.

14
00:01:13.629 --> 00:01:22.829
This is either zero or one, and this is usually used to denote true or false.

15
00:01:23.900 --> 00:01:28.219
And now, below that, we have four green variables.

16
00:01:29.019 --> 00:01:32.219
Now, all of the green ones are numeric.

17
00:01:32.859 --> 00:01:38.700
So the main ones that you're going to encounter are the integer, which is a whole number.

18
00:01:38.700 --> 00:01:41.099
So that could be one, two, three, four, five, six, seven.

19
00:01:41.099 --> 00:01:43.019
It just cannot have any decimal points.

20
00:01:43.900 --> 00:01:49.579
The float, however, can have as many decimal points as you want.

21
00:01:50.219 --> 00:01:55.180
It can be quite a large number, but it takes off a little bit more memory when using it.

22
00:01:56.959 --> 00:02:00.640
Below this, we have these ones in purple and pink.

23
00:02:01.280 --> 00:02:04.159
These are all text-based.

24
00:02:04.159 --> 00:02:08.719
Now, we'd usually use string is probably the most common one of these we'd be using.

25
00:02:09.860 --> 00:02:13.300
Below this, we have vector, rotator and transform.

26
00:02:14.180 --> 00:02:18.740
These are all variables which are often used when working with 3D space.

27
00:02:19.379 --> 00:02:29.219
The vector is three different float variables that would dictate a value in x, y and z.

28
00:02:29.860 --> 00:02:34.180
So it could be used to pinpoint a location in space.

29
00:02:34.819 --> 00:02:37.939
A rotator, again, is three float variables.

30
00:02:38.500 --> 00:02:44.740
However, it has got some extra functionality around working in degrees.

31
00:02:45.710 --> 00:02:51.069
A transform is actually a struct of three different vectors,

32
00:02:51.069 --> 00:02:55.550
a one for location, rotation and scale.

33
00:02:57.199 --> 00:02:58.319
Now, that's not all.

34
00:02:58.319 --> 00:03:03.520
We also have a ton of other variables that are available to us,

35
00:03:03.520 --> 00:03:05.599
and these can be numerous different things.

36
00:03:06.400 --> 00:03:10.080
One of the things that we very often use is references.

37
00:03:10.080 --> 00:03:12.479
So say I wanted a reference to an actor.

38
00:03:12.560 --> 00:03:16.319
I could just type in actor, and we'll be able to see here

39
00:03:16.319 --> 00:03:20.319
we can have an object reference to a specific actor in blue.

40
00:03:21.539 --> 00:03:24.659
In this case, I'm just going to use a transform

41
00:03:24.659 --> 00:03:27.219
so that we can look into this a bit more.

42
00:03:28.419 --> 00:03:34.659
Now, the first thing is we do have a setting for default value

43
00:03:34.659 --> 00:03:36.900
down here in the bottom right hand corner.

44
00:03:36.900 --> 00:03:40.259
You cannot set this until we have actually gone away and compiled.

45
00:03:40.259 --> 00:03:44.900
So you can see now that I've compiled this, I can go in here,

46
00:03:44.900 --> 00:03:49.300
and I've got my three different locations, and I can set this,

47
00:03:49.300 --> 00:03:51.860
and then that's going to be the default value that is set

48
00:03:53.300 --> 00:03:55.539
at the beginning when any of these are created.

49
00:03:56.979 --> 00:04:03.919
So let's look at how a variable will be used within the event graph.

50
00:04:04.770 --> 00:04:08.050
Now, if I come down and grab one of the variables

51
00:04:08.050 --> 00:04:12.930
and drag it into the C, and then just left-hand click to drop it,

52
00:04:13.979 --> 00:04:18.139
you're going to see that we get two different options, a GET or a SET.

53
00:04:18.939 --> 00:04:26.740
Now, what these are is a GET will return the value

54
00:04:26.740 --> 00:04:35.170
which is stored within the variable, and a SET is going to overwrite the value

55
00:04:35.170 --> 00:04:37.649
which is in that variable.

56
00:04:37.649 --> 00:04:43.139
So a GET is going to return the value, and a SET is going to change it.

57
00:04:43.139 --> 00:04:48.660
So if I select GET, you'll see that I've now got a node here,

58
00:04:48.660 --> 00:04:51.939
which on the right hand side has an output pin

59
00:04:52.899 --> 00:04:56.899
that is going to provide us with a transform.

60
00:04:58.160 --> 00:05:03.120
Now, if I drag one in again, and this time use SET,

61
00:05:04.050 --> 00:05:06.769
you're going to see that we have an execution pin.

62
00:05:07.360 --> 00:05:11.439
That's because we're not just querying this, we're actually going to change it.

63
00:05:11.439 --> 00:05:14.800
So there is an action that's going to be done,

64
00:05:14.800 --> 00:05:18.319
and that needs to be done at a certain time and a certain place.

65
00:05:18.319 --> 00:05:23.199
So we'll need to have an input, and we have an output pin accordingly.

66
00:05:23.199 --> 00:05:30.240
Now, what we plug into this right-hand side is going to be what is overwritten.

67
00:05:30.240 --> 00:05:34.480
What comes out of the left-hand side, what comes out of the right-hand side

68
00:05:34.480 --> 00:05:37.680
is going to be the value, the end value.

69
00:05:38.980 --> 00:05:42.990
Now, there is a quicker way to bring these in.

70
00:05:43.709 --> 00:05:45.310
I just delete these ones here.

71
00:05:46.110 --> 00:05:52.269
If I was to drag in a new file, and if I was to hold down the CTRL key when I drop it,

72
00:05:52.269 --> 00:05:54.670
it's automatically going to give us a GET.

73
00:05:55.470 --> 00:05:58.110
If I drag it in and hold down the ALT key,

74
00:05:58.829 --> 00:06:00.829
it's going to automatically give us a SET.

75
00:06:02.459 --> 00:06:05.100
Now, there is another thing.

76
00:06:05.100 --> 00:06:12.399
Because this is a struct, we are going to be capable of right-hand clicking,

77
00:06:12.399 --> 00:06:15.439
and we've got an option to split the struct pin.

78
00:06:16.350 --> 00:06:19.310
What that's going to do is it's going to break it down

79
00:06:19.310 --> 00:06:26.269
so that we can now have a pin for each one of the three vectors.

80
00:06:26.269 --> 00:06:28.670
So location, rotation, and scale.

81
00:06:29.230 --> 00:06:31.310
We can do this on the opposite end as well.

82
00:06:31.949 --> 00:06:33.310
Split the stroke pin.

83
00:06:33.310 --> 00:06:39.220
And that's useful if you only want to get hold of the location, for example, or rotation.

84
00:06:40.209 --> 00:06:46.129
And we can even split this further into the pure floats.

85
00:06:46.129 --> 00:06:47.889
So I could split them.

86
00:06:47.889 --> 00:06:51.970
If I just wanted to get the Z location, which would show us how high up

87
00:06:51.970 --> 00:06:56.769
this transform is in the world, then I can access just that float.

88
00:06:59.439 --> 00:07:02.959
And this is common in lots of other modes as well.

89
00:07:03.120 --> 00:07:10.750
If I was to grab a trace, the line traced by channel,

90
00:07:12.529 --> 00:07:16.610
you can see here on the right-hand side, I have an OUT hit, which is a struct.

91
00:07:17.170 --> 00:07:20.129
And if I right-hand click on that and split that struct pin,

92
00:07:20.129 --> 00:07:22.129
you're going to get a lot more information.

93
00:07:23.089 --> 00:07:26.769
So be aware that you can do that at any one point.

94
00:07:29.889 --> 00:07:33.250
You can also right-hand click and go re-combine struct pins

95
00:07:34.050 --> 00:07:38.100
if you want to re-combine them.

96
00:07:38.100 --> 00:07:42.259
If you ever need to create a variable, there is one other option as well.

97
00:07:42.259 --> 00:07:48.819
Say, for example, in this case, I wanted to save the OUT hit actor.

98
00:07:49.459 --> 00:07:53.860
Now, a quick way that I can do this is I could right-hand click on the output pin

99
00:07:53.860 --> 00:07:55.779
and go Promote to Variable.

100
00:07:56.420 --> 00:08:03.379
And that will automatically create the variable which could contain this value.

101
00:08:03.939 --> 00:08:08.420
And this is quite useful because you sometimes might be confused

102
00:08:08.420 --> 00:08:11.939
what variable type you are working with.

103
00:08:12.660 --> 00:08:17.220
But if you do it this way, you're going to know that that's going to connect up correctly.

104
00:08:18.430 --> 00:08:20.110
So that is variables.

105
00:08:20.110 --> 00:08:24.670
We'll be playing with these a bit more when we jump into the exercise.

106
00:08:24.670 --> 00:08:27.550
But just be aware of how they work.
